// **The pond, stocked** -- the measurement bed for every aquatic brief in
// `Reports/aquatic-implementation-plan-2026-09-14.md`.
//
// `the_pond.ron` asks whether a placed body of water holds. It does: 4,256
// cells, exactly constant to frame 40,000. This file is that basin with a
// bed around it -- plants on both banks and a colony on the near one -- so
// the question becomes ecological rather than hydraulic.
//
// **The colony arrives at frame 6,000, not at frame 0, and that is the whole
// reason this is a separate file.** `Reports/evolution-lab-direction-2026-09-
// 09.md` records the owner's correction: the harness bed is not the played
// bed, and a colony dropped onto seedlings at frame 0 collapses at once and
// never recovers, where one founded on a grown bed holds. Measured there,
// same seed: 5 ants at frame 6,000 founded-at-zero against 39 founded-at-
// six-thousand. A pond arm measured against a frame-0 colony would be
// measuring that collapse, not the water.
//
// **Food is on the far bank and the colony is on the near one**, so the pond
// stands between the animals and half the larder. That was authored as the
// reachability experiment and **it is not one**. Three arms, six seeds each,
// frame 90,000, colony arriving at 6,000, ants alive at the end: flat bed
// median 1.0, this bed 2.0, the same pit left *dry* 2.5. Water is not
// distinguishable from the same hole left dry, and what little a pit buys, it
// buys dry. (A one-seed reading of the same arms said 3 / 4 / 28; the 28 was
// the outlier of the whole sweep, which is what one seed looks like here.)
//
// **And every arm ends with one to three ants out of 52 founders**, so this
// bed at this horizon has almost no power to discriminate anything. Do not
// read it as "the pond is spatial structure", and do not measure a swim verb
// by colony size in it -- measure the verb. The plan's §1.4 has the table.
//
// Two herbs stand on the near bank as well as four on the far one, so a
// colony that never crosses is not simply starved by placement.
(
    name: "the_pond_stocked",
    title: "THE POND, STOCKED",
    question: "A colony on one bank, most of the larder on the other, and a pond between them -- does the water change anything the bed would not have done anyway?",
    bed: (
        width: 512,
        height: 320,
        soil_depth: 96,
        ground_y: 160,
        compartments: 1,
        founders: 0,
        species: "herb",
        colonies: 0,
        colony_ants: 52,
        colony_species: "ant",
        predators: 0,
        lamp_spacing: 33,
        seed: 1,
        extra_walls: [],
    ),
    settings: [],
    placements: [
        Clear(x: 176, y: 160, w: 160, h: 36),
        Fill(material: "stone", x: 176, y: 160, w: 4, h: 36),
        Fill(material: "stone", x: 332, y: 160, w: 4, h: 36),
        Fill(material: "stone", x: 176, y: 192, w: 160, h: 4),
        Fill(material: "water", x: 180, y: 164, w: 152, h: 28),
        // The far bank: most of the larder.
        Plant(species: "herb", x: 360),
        Plant(species: "herb", x: 400),
        Plant(species: "herb", x: 440),
        Plant(species: "herb", x: 480),
        // The near bank, so the colony is not merely stranded.
        Plant(species: "herb", x: 60),
        Plant(species: "herb", x: 100),
    ],
    timeline: [
        (at: 6000, every: 0, until: 0, what: Colony(species: "ant", x: 90, count: 52)),
    ],
    horizon: 90000,
)
